  <h2>Problem</h2>
  <p>
    Suppliers list products on their own e-commerce sites in their own formats. WooCommerce-based stores want continuously synced catalogues, but the practical reality is one-off CSV imports done by hand, no ongoing sync, and no normalised product metadata. Every onboarding cycle starts from zero.
  </p>
  <p>
    ColorGirl automates both the initial import <em>and</em> the continuous sync. The initial scrape sells the trial. The sync sells the subscription.
  </p>

  <h2>Architecture</h2>
  <p>A 9-phase Temporal workflow orchestrates the pipeline end-to-end, with human-in-the-loop approval gates at each phase boundary:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Reconnaissance</strong>: Claude Opus profiles the supplier site, infers structure, and proposes a scraping strategy.</li>
    <li><strong>Tier selection</strong>: Opus selects between HTTP+cheerio (~100–200 products/min) for static sites, or Playwright (~12 products/min) for JavaScript-rendered SPAs.</li>
    <li><strong>Config generation</strong>: produces the scraper config the worker needs to actually crawl.</li>
    <li><strong>Crawl</strong>: typed product extraction with retry semantics under Temporal.</li>
    <li><strong>Image pipeline</strong>: fetch, normalise, and stage assets for the destination store.</li>
    <li><strong>SEO metadata</strong>: Claude Sonnet writes titles, slugs, and structured metadata.</li>
    <li><strong>Descriptions</strong>: Claude Haiku generates batched product descriptions at the cheapest tier where quality is enough.</li>
    <li><strong>Approval gate</strong>: operator reviews via React/Ink TUI; signals approval back to the workflow.</li>
    <li><strong>Push</strong>: WooCommerce-ready CSV (or platform-native via the SaaS roadmap: Shopify, BigCommerce, Magento).</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Approach</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Multi-model routing.</strong> Opus for the hard reasoning (site reconnaissance, config inference). Sonnet for the structured-output step (SEO metadata). Haiku for the cheap, parallelisable bulk (product descriptions, batched). Per-import compute cost lands in cents, not dollars.</li>
    <li><strong>Temporal as the spine.</strong> Long-running scrapes can't be a single fragile process. Temporal handles retries, durable state and signal-based human-in-the-loop approvals: the operator can step away mid-run without losing work.</li>
    <li><strong>Auto-tier selection.</strong> Static sites scrape at 100–200 products/min via HTTP+cheerio. JS-rendered SPAs fall back to Playwright at ~12 products/min. The choice is made once, by Opus, during analysis (not hard-coded).</li>
    <li><strong>Tested pipeline.</strong> Vitest covers the orchestration; tsup bundles a CLI; the React/Ink TUI gives the operator a real terminal UI rather than a wall of logs.</li>
    <li><strong>OSS CLI + commercial SaaS.</strong> The CLI is AGPL-3.0. The hosted SaaS (in beta) layers on a shared supplier registry: a network effect where new users onboard in minutes against suppliers someone else already characterised.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Outcome</h2>
  <p>
    The CLI is live; the SaaS dashboard is in beta. Sprint roadmap covers supplier registry, multi-store push, image sync engine, Stripe billing, and Shopify/BigCommerce/Magento adapters. Pricing target ~AUD $30/month with margin held by the multi-model routing keeping per-import cost in cents.
  </p>

